1
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:18,640
Hey folks, where we left off I was

2
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:19,960
talking about how to compute a

3
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:22,120
three-dimensional cross product between

4
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,360
two vectors, V cross W.

5
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:26,920
It's this funny thing where you write a

6
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:28,840
matrix whose second column has the

7
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:31,160
coordinates of V, whose third column has

8
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,480
the coordinates of W, but the entries of

9
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:35,760
that first column, weirdly, are the

10
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,960
symbols I hat, J hat, and K hat, where

11
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:40,600
you just pretend like those guys are

12
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,800
numbers for the sake of computations.

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:45,880
Then, with that funky matrix in hand,

14
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:48,120
you compute its determinant.

15
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:49,400
If you just chug along with those

16
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:51,800
computations, ignoring the weirdness,

17
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,280
you get some constant times I hat plus

18
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:56,440
some constant times J hat plus some

19
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:59,680
constant times K hat.

20
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:01,400
How specifically you think about

21
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,400
computing that determinant is kind of

22
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:05,199
beside the point. All that really

23
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:06,800
matters here is that you'll end up with

24
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:08,360
three different numbers that are

25
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,280
interpreted as the coordinates of some

26
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:13,320
resulting vector.

27
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,520
From here, students are typically told

28
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:17,320
to just believe that the resulting

29
00:01:17,320 --> 00:01:19,440
vector has the following geometric

30
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:20,680
properties.

31
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:22,560
Its length equals the area of the

32
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:25,480
parallelogram defined by V and W.

33
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:27,560
It points in a direction perpendicular

34
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:30,000
to both V and W. And this direction

35
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,160
obeys the right-hand rule, in the sense

36
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:34,040
that if you point your forefinger along

37
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:37,200
V and your middle finger along W, then

38
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,840
when you stick up your thumb, it'll

39
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:40,440
point in the direction of the new

40
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:42,960
vector.

41
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:45,760
There are some brute-force computations

42
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:47,040
that you could do to confirm these

43
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:48,840
facts, but I want to share with you a

44
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:51,120
really elegant line of reasoning.

45
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:53,000
It leverages a bit of background though,

46
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:54,480
so for this video I'm assuming that

47
00:01:54,480 --> 00:01:56,480
everybody has watched chapter five on

48
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:58,800
the determinant and chapter 7 where I

49
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:02,480
introduce the idea of duality.

50
00:02:04,640 --> 00:02:07,280
As a quick reminder, the idea of duality

51
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:08,880
is that anytime you have a linear

52
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:10,960
transformation from some space to the

53
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:13,000
number line, it's associated with a

54
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,720
unique vector in that space in the sense

55
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:16,959
that performing the linear

56
00:02:16,959 --> 00:02:19,240
transformation is the same as taking a

57
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:21,959
dot product with that vector.

58
00:02:21,959 --> 00:02:23,760
Numerically, this is because one of

59
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:26,000
those transformations is described by a

60
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:28,440
matrix with just one row where each

61
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:30,160
column tells you the number that each

62
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:33,520
basis vector lands on.

63
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:36,880
And multiplying this matrix by some

64
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:40,280
vector V is computationally identical to

65
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,480
taking the dot product between V and the

66
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:44,400
vector you get by turning that matrix on

67
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,480
its side.

68
00:02:46,480 --> 00:02:48,160
The takeaway is that whenever you're out

69
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:50,160
in the mathematical wild and you find a

70
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:51,760
linear transformation to the number

71
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,080
line, you will be able to match it to

72
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,440
some vector, which is called the dual

73
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,640
vector of that transformation. So that

74
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,920
performing the linear transformation is

75
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:02,720
the same as taking a dot product with

76
00:03:02,720 --> 00:03:05,480
that vector.

77
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:08,000
The cross product gives us a really

78
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,400
slick example of this process in action.

79
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:11,760
It takes some effort, but it's

80
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:13,519
definitely worth it.

81
00:03:13,519 --> 00:03:15,519
What I'm going to do is define a certain

82
00:03:15,519 --> 00:03:17,280
linear transformation from three

83
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:19,440
dimensions to the number line and it'll

84
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:21,680
be defined in terms of the two vectors V

85
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:23,080
and W.

86
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:24,760
Then, when we associate that

87
00:03:24,760 --> 00:03:27,320
transformation with its dual vector in

88
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:30,280
3D space, that dual vector is going to

89
00:03:30,280 --> 00:03:33,280
be the cross product of V and W.

90
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:35,040
The reason for doing this will be that

91
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:37,120
understanding that transformation is

92
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:38,959
going to make clear the connection

93
00:03:38,959 --> 00:03:41,600
between the computation and the geometry

94
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,760
of the cross product.

95
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
So, to back up a bit, remember in two

96
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,600
dimensions what it meant to compute the

97
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:50,880
2D version of the cross product?

98
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:53,360
When you have two vectors V and W, you

99
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:55,400
put the coordinates of V as the first

100
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:57,320
column of a matrix and the coordinates

101
00:03:57,320 --> 00:03:59,720
of W as the second column of a matrix.

102
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:01,680
Then you just compute the determinant.

103
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:03,520
There's no nonsense with basis vectors

104
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:05,440
stuck in a matrix or anything like that,

105
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:07,480
just an ordinary determinant returning a

106
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:09,120
number.

107
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:11,520
Geometrically, this gives us the area of

108
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:14,040
a parallelogram spanned out by those two

109
00:04:14,040 --> 00:04:16,320
vectors with the possibility of being

110
00:04:16,320 --> 00:04:18,120
negative depending on the orientation of

111
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:19,680
the vectors.

112
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:21,480
Now, if you didn't already know the 3D

113
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:22,760
cross product and you're trying to

114
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:25,040
extrapolate, you might imagine that it

115
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:26,919
involves taking three separate 3D

116
00:04:26,919 --> 00:04:30,120
vectors U, V, and W and making their

117
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:34,880
coordinates the columns of a 3x3 matrix.

118
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:36,880
Then computing the determinant of that

119
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:38,840
matrix.

120
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:41,000
And as you know from chapter five,

121
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
geometrically, this would give you the

122
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,960
volume of a parallelepiped spanned out

123
00:04:45,960 --> 00:04:48,440
by those three vectors with a plus or

124
00:04:48,440 --> 00:04:50,120
minus sign depending on the right-hand

125
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:52,960
rule orientation of those three vectors.

126
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:54,760
Of course, you all know that this is not

127
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:57,160
the 3D cross product.

128
00:04:57,160 --> 00:04:59,560
The actual 3D cross product takes in two

129
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:02,520
vectors and spits out a vector.

130
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:04,160
It doesn't take in three vectors and

131
00:05:04,160 --> 00:05:06,400
spit out a number. But this idea

132
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:08,040
actually gets us really close to what

133
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:10,800
the real cross product is.

134
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:12,720
Consider that first vector U to be a

135
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:15,200
variable, say with variable entries X,

136
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:16,560
Y, and Z

137
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:20,400
while V and W remain fixed.

138
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:25,000
What we have then is a function from

139
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,120
three dimensions to the number line. You

140
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:30,120
input some vector XYZ and you get out a

141
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:32,200
number by taking the determinant of a

142
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:35,400
matrix whose first column is XYZ

143
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:37,160
and whose other two columns are the

144
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:39,440
coordinates of the constant vectors V

145
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:40,680
and W.

146
00:05:40,680 --> 00:05:42,360
Geometrically, the meaning of this

147
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:44,840
function is that for any input vector

148
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:48,080
XYZ, you consider the parallelepiped

149
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:51,600
defined by this vector V and W. Then you

150
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:54,040
return its volume with a plus or minus

151
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:57,400
sign depending on orientation.

152
00:05:57,400 --> 00:05:58,720
Now, this might feel like kind of a

153
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:00,720
random thing to do. I mean, where does

154
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:02,040
this function come from? Why are we

155
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:03,840
defining it this way?

156
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:05,080
And I'll admit, at this stage it might

157
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:06,320
kind of feel like it's coming out of the

158
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:08,160
blue. But if you're willing to go along

159
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:09,360
with it and play around with the

160
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,320
properties that this guy has, it's the

161
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:15,520
key to understanding the cross product.

162
00:06:15,520 --> 00:06:17,400
One really important fact about this

163
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,880
function is that it's linear.

164
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:21,640
I'll actually leave it to you to work

165
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:23,480
through the details of why this is true

166
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:26,320
based on properties of the determinant.

167
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:28,080
But once you know that it's linear, we

168
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:29,680
can start bringing in the idea of

169
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:32,400
duality.

170
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:36,880
Once you know that it's linear, you know

171
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:38,600
that there's some way to describe this

172
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:41,400
function as matrix multiplication.

173
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:43,160
Specifically, since it's a function that

174
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:45,080
goes from three dimensions to one

175
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:48,200
dimension, there will be a 1 by 3 matrix

176
00:06:48,200 --> 00:06:52,040
that encodes this transformation.

177
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:55,400
And the whole idea of duality is that

178
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:57,120
the special thing about transformations

179
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,360
from several dimensions to one dimension

180
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:01,080
is that you can turn that matrix on its

181
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,240
side and instead interpret the entire

182
00:07:03,240 --> 00:07:05,640
transformation as the dot product with a

183
00:07:05,640 --> 00:07:08,560
certain vector.

184
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:10,680
What we're looking for is the special 3D

185
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:13,600
vector that I'll call P such that taking

186
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,000
the dot product between P and any other

187
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:19,120
vector XYZ gives the same result as

188
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:21,919
plugging in XYZ as the first column of a

189
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:24,720
3 by 3 matrix whose other two columns

190
00:07:24,720 --> 00:07:27,000
have the coordinates of V and W, then

191
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,080
computing the determinant.

192
00:07:29,080 --> 00:07:30,640
I'll get to the geometry of this in just

193
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:32,800
a moment, but right now let's dig in and

194
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:33,840
think about what this means

195
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:36,120
computationally.

196
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:39,320
Taking the dot product between P and XYZ

197
00:07:39,320 --> 00:07:41,440
will give us something times X plus

198
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:44,400
something times Y plus something times Z

199
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:46,080
where those somethings are the

200
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:47,880
coordinates of P.

201
00:07:47,880 --> 00:07:49,400
But on the right side here, when you

202
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:51,320
compute the determinant, you can

203
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,240
organize it to look like some constant

204
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:56,320
times X plus some constant times Y plus

205
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:58,320
some constant times Z.

206
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:00,440
Where those constants involve certain

207
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:02,680
combinations of the components of V and

208
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:04,240
W.

209
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:06,320
So those constants, those particular

210
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:07,960
combinations of the coordinates of V and

211
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:10,960
W are going to be the coordinates of the

212
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,720
vector P that we're looking for.

213
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:20,120
But what's going on on the right here

214
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:21,919
should feel very familiar to anyone

215
00:08:21,919 --> 00:08:23,480
who's actually worked through a cross

216
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:25,800
product computation.

217
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:27,320
Collecting the constant terms that are

218
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:30,400
multiplied by X, Y, and by Z like this

219
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:31,960
is no different from plugging in the

220
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:34,960
symbols I hat, J hat, and K hat to that

221
00:08:34,960 --> 00:08:36,919
first column and seeing which

222
00:08:36,919 --> 00:08:38,919
coefficients aggregate on each one of

223
00:08:38,919 --> 00:08:40,840
those terms.

224
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:43,039
It's just that plugging in I hat, J hat,

225
00:08:43,039 --> 00:08:45,480
and K hat is a way of signaling that we

226
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:47,680
should interpret those coefficients as

227
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:51,200
the coordinates of a vector.

228
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,000
So what all of this is saying is that

229
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,280
this funky computation can be thought of

230
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:56,680
as a way to answer the following

231
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:57,880
question.

232
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:00,839
What vector P has the special property

233
00:09:00,839 --> 00:09:02,600
that when you take a dot product between

234
00:09:02,600 --> 00:09:06,320
P and some vector XYZ, it gives the same

235
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:09,120
result as plugging in XYZ to the first

236
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:11,080
column of a matrix whose other two

237
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:13,680
columns have the coordinates of V and W,

238
00:09:13,680 --> 00:09:15,839
then computing the determinant.

239
00:09:15,839 --> 00:09:17,640
That's a bit of a mouthful, but it's an

240
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:19,280
important question to digest for this

241
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:21,320
video.

242
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:23,080
Now for the cool part which ties all

243
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:24,560
this together with the geometric

244
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:26,280
understanding of the cross product that

245
00:09:26,280 --> 00:09:28,920
I introduced last video.

246
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:30,280
I'm going to ask the same question

247
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:32,160
again, but this time we're going to try

248
00:09:32,160 --> 00:09:34,120
to answer it geometrically instead of

249
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:36,520
computationally.

250
00:09:36,520 --> 00:09:39,000
What 3D vector P has the special

251
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:40,800
property that when you take a dot

252
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:43,440
product between P and some other vector

253
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:46,720
XYZ, it gives the same result as if you

254
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:48,320
took the signed volume of a

255
00:09:48,320 --> 00:09:51,280
parallelepiped defined by this vector

256
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:56,160
XYZ along with V and W.

257
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:59,520
Remember, the geometric interpretation

258
00:09:59,520 --> 00:10:02,160
of a dot product between a vector P and

259
00:10:02,160 --> 00:10:04,600
some other vector is to project that

260
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:07,560
other vector onto P, then to multiply

261
00:10:07,560 --> 00:10:09,680
the length of that projection by the

262
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:12,480
length of P.

263
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:15,600
With that in mind, let me show a certain

264
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:17,320
way to think about the volume of the

265
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:20,240
parallelepiped that we care about.

266
00:10:20,240 --> 00:10:22,000
Start by taking the area of the

267
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
parallelogram defined by V and W,

268
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,040
then multiply it not by the length of

269
00:10:27,040 --> 00:10:30,600
XYZ, but by the component of XYZ that's

270
00:10:30,600 --> 00:10:34,400
perpendicular to that parallelogram.

271
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:36,240
In other words, the way our linear

272
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:38,520
function works on a given vector is to

273
00:10:38,520 --> 00:10:41,040
project that vector onto a line that's

274
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:43,880
perpendicular to both V and W, then to

275
00:10:43,880 --> 00:10:46,160
multiply the length of that projection

276
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:48,960
by the area of the parallelogram spanned

277
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:51,720
by V and W.

278
00:10:51,720 --> 00:10:53,400
But this is the same thing as taking a

279
00:10:53,400 --> 00:10:56,400
dot product between XYZ and a vector

280
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:59,240
that's perpendicular to V and W

281
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:00,960
with a length equal to the area of that

282
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:03,200
parallelogram.

283
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:04,680
What's more, if you choose the

284
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:06,680
appropriate direction for that vector,

285
00:11:06,680 --> 00:11:08,280
the cases where the dot product is

286
00:11:08,280 --> 00:11:10,400
negative will line up with the cases

287
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:11,760
where the right-hand rule for the

288
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:14,800
orientation of XYZ, V, and W is

289
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,400
negative.

290
00:11:19,600 --> 00:11:22,320
This means that we just found a vector P

291
00:11:22,320 --> 00:11:24,760
so that taking a dot product between P

292
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:27,920
and some vector XYZ is the same thing as

293
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:30,080
computing that determinant of a 3 by 3

294
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:33,000
matrix whose columns are XYZ, the

295
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,440
coordinates of V and W.

296
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:37,200
So, the answer that we found earlier

297
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:39,280
computationally, using that special

298
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:41,360
notational trick, must correspond

299
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:43,760
geometrically to this vector.

300
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:46,120
This is the fundamental reason why the

301
00:11:46,120 --> 00:11:47,880
computation and the geometric

302
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:49,720
interpretation of the cross product are

303
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:52,480
related.

304
00:11:52,800 --> 00:11:54,560
Just to sum up what happened here, I

305
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:56,200
started by defining a linear

306
00:11:56,200 --> 00:11:58,520
transformation from 3D space to the

307
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:01,000
number line, and it was defined in terms

308
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,280
of the vectors V and W.

309
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:05,160
Then, I went through two separate ways

310
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:07,120
to think about the dual vector of this

311
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:09,160
transformation. The vector such that

312
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:11,400
applying the transformation is the same

313
00:12:11,400 --> 00:12:13,560
thing as taking a dot product with that

314
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:14,760
vector.

315
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:16,560
On the one hand, a computational

316
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:18,600
approach will lead you to the trick of

317
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:20,560
plugging in the symbols I hat, J hat,

318
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:22,320
and K hat to the first column of a

319
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:25,960
matrix and computing the determinant.

320
00:12:25,960 --> 00:12:27,720
But thinking geometrically, we can

321
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:29,960
deduce that this dual vector must be

322
00:12:29,960 --> 00:12:32,760
perpendicular to V and W with a length

323
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:35,280
equal to the area of the parallelogram

324
00:12:35,280 --> 00:12:39,080
spanned out by those two vectors.

325
00:12:39,280 --> 00:12:40,960
Since both of these approaches give us a

326
00:12:40,960 --> 00:12:43,440
dual vector to the same transformation,

327
00:12:43,440 --> 00:12:47,040
they must be the same vector.

328
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:49,080
So, that wraps up dot products and cross

329
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:51,080
products. And the next video will be a

330
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:52,720
really important concept for linear

331
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:56,680
algebra, change of basis.